>
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much do new radiators cost in Formby?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A new radiator supplied and fitted costs from £245 including VAT. Five radiators supplied and fitted cost from £1,195.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table stk stk-price">
          <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Price inc VAT</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>New radiator, supplied and fitted</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">From £245</td><td data-label="Notes">Standard double panel, same spot</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>Five radiators, supplied and fitted</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">From £1,195</td><td data-label="Notes">Usually done in one day</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>Fit your own radiator</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">From £95</td><td data-label="Notes">Same spot, no new pipework</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>Designer or column radiator, fit only</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">From £250</td><td data-label="Notes">Heavier, often wall-mounted brackets</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>Move a radiator</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">From £225</td><td data-label="Notes">Pipework moved to a new wall</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>New radiator in a new position</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">From £325</td><td data-label="Notes">Extensions and rooms without one</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>TRV fitted</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">£65 each, or up to 6 for £295</td><td data-label="Notes">Set the heat room by room</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>Radiator repairs and leaks</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">First hour £120</td><td data-label="Notes">£150 evenings and weekends, £180 bank holidays</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What is included</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>A heat check for the room before we suggest a size</li>
        <li>The new radiator and all fittings (supply and fit jobs)</li>
        <li>Old radiator taken off and taken away</li>
        <li>Inhibitor checked and topped up</li>
        <li>System refilled, bled and balanced</li>
        <li>A 12-month workmanship guarantee</li>
      </ul>
      <p>There is no call-out fee, and the quote visit is free. A straight swap can often be priced on the phone.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="sizing">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Why is my Formby room always cold?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Most often, the radiator is too small. Formby has some of the biggest rooms in Merseyside, and many have a radiator sized for an average room.</p>
      <p>A sitting room in Freshfield or Formby Village may have a high ceiling and a big bay window. That room loses a lot of heat. A standard radiator that suits a normal room will not keep up. The heating can run all day and the room still will not warm.</p>
      <p>Fitting the same size again will not fix it. So we work out the heat each room needs first. We look at:</p>
      <ul class="brx-list">
        <li>The floor area and the ceiling height</li>
        <li>The size of the windows, and whether they are double glazed</li>
        <li>How many walls face outside, and the insulation</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Then we pick a radiator with enough output. We never size by what was there before, or by what fits the wall.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="column">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Should I fit a column radiator in a bay window?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Often, yes. A tall column radiator under the bay suits a period room and warms the cold air from the glass before it spreads.</p>
      <p>A bay window is a lovely feature, but it lets in a lot of cold. A radiator on a side wall leaves the bay cold. A radiator of the right size in the bay works much better.</p>
      <p>We fit two, three and four-column radiators, up to 1800mm tall for high ceilings. We size the height and width for the heat the room needs. The look comes second. Where we can, we run pipes under the timber floor to keep the room neat.</p>
      <h3>Other radiator types</h3>
      <ul class="brx-list">
        <li><strong>Panel radiators.</strong> Best for smaller rooms. Double panels give the most heat for the money.</li>
        <li><strong>Designer and vertical radiators.</strong> Good in open-plan kitchens where wall space is tight. Many give less heat, so we check the output first.</li>
        <li><strong>Towel rails.</strong> In chrome, white or anthracite. A dual-fuel rail needs a safe electrical supply. Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/">Ideal Electrical Solutions</a> can fit that.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Buying online?</strong> Compare the heat output in watts or BTU, not the size. A slim designer radiator may give far less heat than it looks. Tell us the room and we will check it.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="extensions">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Can you add a radiator to my Formby extension?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Yes. A new radiator in a new position is from £325 including VAT. We check the pipe route, the boiler and your heating zones first.</p>
      <p>Many Formby homes have a kitchen extension, a utility room or a rear lounge. If it has no underfloor heating, it needs a radiator on the main circuit. We size it for the new room. We also check the boiler can heat it.</p>
      <h3>Homes with two heating zones</h3>
      <p>Larger Formby homes often have separate upstairs and downstairs zones. Each has its own valve and thermostat. A new radiator on the wrong zone can be always on or always cold. We connect it to the right zone, and set the controls so the room heats when you want it.</p>
      <h3>TRVs in large homes</h3>
      <p>A five-bed house may have sunny rooms that get too hot and north-facing rooms that need more heat. A TRV on each radiator lets each room find its own level. They work with your zone thermostats. TRVs are £65 each, or up to six for £295. We leave one radiator without a TRV, often in the hall, so water can always flow.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does a radiator installation work?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A straight swap takes 1 to 2 hours. Five radiators are usually done in one day.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Free quote visit.</strong> A straight swap can often be priced on the phone. For column radiators, extensions and cold rooms, we visit for free.</li>
        <li><strong>Heat check and fixed written price.</strong> We work out the heat the room needs and pick the radiator. The price covers the radiator, fittings, labour and balancing.</li>
        <li><strong>Drain and remove (30 minutes).</strong> We drain just that radiator where we can. If the valves will not hold, we drain the system.</li>
        <li><strong>Fit the new radiator (30 to 60 minutes).</strong> New brackets go up and the radiator is connected. Floors are covered.</li>
        <li><strong>Refill and balance (30 minutes).</strong> We top up the inhibitor, refill and bleed. Then we balance every radiator, zone by zone.</li>
        <li><strong>Old radiator taken away.</strong> We tidy up before we leave.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Need new radiators in Formby?</strong> Call for a free quote, from £245 including VAT.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
      <h3>Do I need a power flush first?</h3>
      <p>If several radiators are cold at the bottom, yes. Sludge will soon clog new radiators. It can also harm your boiler. If they heat well and the water is clear, there is no need. See <a href="/power-flushing-formby">power flushing in Formby</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="formby">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Radiator repairs and areas we cover in Formby</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We repair and replace radiators across Formby L37 and nearby Sefton. Repairs are from £120 for the first hour on weekdays 9am to 4pm, with no call-out fee.</p>
      <p>The first hour is £150 evenings, nights and weekends, and £180 on bank holidays. If a job needs more time, we tell you the price before we carry on.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>Leaking valve.</strong> Often a loose gland nut or worn valve. Usually a quick fix.</li>
        <li><strong>Stone cold.</strong> Often a stuck TRV pin or a closed valve.</li>
        <li><strong>One zone weaker than the other.</strong> Sludge in some radiators, or a zone that needs balancing. We find the cause first.</li>
        <li><strong>Rust or pinholes.</strong> The radiator is rusting from inside. Replace it, as the rust spreads sludge round the system.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="brx-danger"><p><strong>Radiator leaking now?</strong> Put a bowl and towels under it. Turn both valves clockwise to close them. If it will not stop, turn the water off at the stopcock and call <a href="tel:01515580334">{PHONE}</a>.</p></div>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Postcode</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Formby, Formby Village, Freshfield</strong></td><td>L37</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Hightown</strong></td><td>L38</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Crosby</strong></td><td>L23</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Maghull</strong></td><td>L31</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Ainsdale</strong></td><td>PR8</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Our heating engineers are part of our team led by James. All work on the heating circuit is done by our Gas Safe registered engineers. You can check any engineer on the <a href="https://www.gassaferegister.co.uk">Gas Safe Register</a>. We carry £2 million public liability insurance.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
